    feat(scripts): rename-aware upgrade-path check (QA-LORE-RELENG-1)
    
    The QA upgrade cell installs the previous release from the package index and
    therefore cannot pass: get-h3-lore is deliberately unpublished on PyPI
    (README/INSTALL state the posture), so "upgrade path untestable" was measuring
    a channel this project does not have. This closes the testable half of that
    gap on the channel that does exist, and fixes two instructions that were wrong.
    
    scripts/upgrade-path-check.sh (offline, 4 rungs, ~3s):
      1 v0.1.0-install          tag v0.1.0 installs as distribution `lore` 0.1.0
      2 naive-upgrade-leftover  installing the current tree over it leaves BOTH
                                dists installed -> migration required (observed)
      3 rename-migration        uninstall `lore` then install -> one dist, 0.2.0
      4 post-upgrade-verify     CLI smoke + installed __version__ really swapped
    
    The rename is what makes this non-obvious: `lore` -> `get-h3-lore` changes the
    distribution identity, so a plain install is not an upgrade and uv records the
    tool under the new name. Verified live: `uv tool upgrade lore` answers
    "`lore` is not installed"; the tool lists as `get-h3-lore v0.2.0` and
    `uv tool upgrade get-h3-lore` is the working form. README and docs/INSTALL.md
    now carry the migration instead of the stale `uv tool upgrade lore`.
    
    Evidence: docs/release-evidence/upgrade-path-2026-11-04.txt (this tree,
    v0.1.0 tag c655666, RESULT: PASS rungs=4).
    
    Gates: pytest 566 passed; ruff check + format clean; check-test-count PASS
    (canonical=566 live=566, class-count=13).
    
    Residual (not this row): index publication remains gated by
    RELEASE-LORE-006 — nothing is published under get-h3-lore, so the QA cell's
    index-based premise needs changing on the harness side, not in this repo.
